So, for example, you don't want to just say, I'm in favor of approving the project because it would not be substantially more detrimental.

1060
01:34:46,125 --> 01:34:46,826
You want to say why?

1061
01:34:46,866 --> 01:34:50,908
You want to say this would not be substantially more detrimental because this specific

1062
01:34:51,188 --> 01:35:01,155
project does or accomplishes these things and, you know, make it specific to the project that makes it a much more, you know, thoughtful and defensible decision were someone to repeal it.

1063
01:35:01,696 --> 01:35:08,100
And it really is best to not just kind of repeat the standard or repeat the statutory standards, but you want to give your reasons.

1064
01:35:08,781 --> 01:35:11,423
You want to give your specific reasons for approval if there are

1065
01:35:11,881 --> 01:35:13,721
specific requirements in the bylaw.

1066
01:35:13,741 --> 01:35:17,682
It says in order for the board to grant a special permit, it must find as follows.

1067
01:35:17,742 --> 01:35:22,343
You want to identify those things and say why you have found, that you have found each of those things and why.

1068
01:35:23,804 --> 01:35:38,107
You don't need to give as much detail on a denial, but it is still very helpful for boards to explain their reasons to vote their findings for denials so that if that's appealed, well, first of all, in fairness to applicants, they should understand why something

1069
01:35:38,587 --> 01:35:46,692
passed or failed, and in fairness to all butters in the public, the same thing, but also for being able to defend decisions if they're appealed and they go to court.

1070
01:35:47,012 --> 01:35:54,096
You know, as much as it's a de novo and new review, the more specifics that are in there, the better we can represent the board.

1071
01:35:54,316 --> 01:36:01,700
So, I mean, all around, there's a number of reasons to put that level of specificity in and be project specific.

1072
01:36:05,817 --> 01:36:29,784
I know I've mentioned this as well, it's a little bit more detail, but you can impose conditions on special permits and that can be on time and use, hours of operation, even sometimes renewal of permits, deadlines to start or complete certain work, limitations on noise or types of landscaping.

1073
01:36:30,065 --> 01:36:31,225
There's a good amount of leeway,

1074
01:36:32,106 --> 01:36:37,512
In the type of conditions that you can impose on a special permit, they just need to be reasonable, they need to have a basis in zoning.

1075
01:36:41,737 --> 01:36:47,864
And special permits can be limited in duration to ownership or use by a particular applicant.

1076
01:36:48,872 --> 01:36:54,534
Again, if you're considering doing that in a particular application sometimes it's helpful to talk to counsel and just flush that out a little bit.

1077
01:36:56,154 --> 01:37:11,178
Something I'll note in this Victor, and you may know this may have changed with your update but 48 section nine specifies that zoning ordinances and bylaws can specify that special permits will lapse if they have not been used.

1078
01:37:11,638 --> 01:37:12,919
within up to three years.

